{{was wotd|2010|June|6}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno}} {{term|redounder|lang=fro}}, {{etyl|frm}} {{term|redonder|lang=frm}}, and their source, {{etyl|la}} {{term|redundo|rēdundō|lang=la}}, from {{term|re|rē|lang=la}} + {{term|undo|undō|surge|lang=la}}, from {{term|unda||a wave|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ɹɪˈdaʊnd/|/ɹəˈdaʊnd/}}
* {{rhymes|aʊnd}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{obsolete|intransitive}} To [[swell]] up (of water, waves etc.); to [[overflow]], to [[surge]] (of bodily fluids). {{defdate|14th-19th c.}}
#* '''1596''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''The Faerie Queene'', IV.10:
#*: For every dram of hony therein found / A pound of gall doth over it '''redound''' […].
# {{intransitive}} To [[contribute]] {{term|to}} an [[advantage]] or [[disadvantage]] for someone or something. {{defdate|from 15th c.}} 
#* '''1970''', Alvin Toffler, ''Future Shock'', Bantam Books, p. 448:
#*: The fact that in one case the advance '''redounds''' to private advantage and in the other, theoretically, to the public good, does not alter the core assumptions common to both.
# {{intransitive}} To [[contribute]] {{term|to}} the honour, shame etc. of a person or organisation. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''2008''', {{w|Peter Preston}}, ''The Observer'', 2 Mar 2008:
#*: One thing about the 'John McCain-didn't-sleep-with-a-lobbyist' story '''redounds''' to the New York Times' credit.
# {{intransitive}} To [[reverberate]], to [[echo]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{transitive}} To [[reflect]] (honour, shame etc.) {{term|to}} or {{term|onto}} someone. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{intransitive}} To attach, come back, accrue {{term|to}} someone; to reflect back {{term|on}} or {{term|upon}} someone (of honour, shame etc.). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: ''His infamous behaviour only '''redounded''' back upon him when he was caught.''
# {{intransitive}} To [[arise]] {{term|from}} or {{term|out of}} something). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}

